## Goal
Flag a call whose argument types match no overload of the callee — specifically handle arguments whose class/interface does not implicitly convert to any candidate parameter type.

## Source
tm-dips-plus-plus `src/Minigames/HeadPose.as` (2026-09-03). Two instances in one compile; both silent under `openplanet-lsp check` (gate passed, 0 errors), both hard errors in-game. Both the callee and the call site are plugin-local declarations — no dependency injection involved.

Instance 1: `uint Count(NSmPlayerVis_SMgr@ mgr)`-style helper called with an interface handle:
```angelscript
// declared: uint SmPlayerVis::PlayersLength(NSmPlayerVis_SMgr@ mgr)
auto smMgr = SmPlayerVis::GetMgr(app.GameScene); // SmPlayerVis::ISmPlayerVisMgr@
uint n = SmPlayerVis::PlayersLength(smMgr);      // ICounter@ -> RawMgr@ does not convert
```

Instance 2: parameter is `CMwNod@`, argument is a game class that is not a nod:
```angelscript
// declared: uint64 Kinematic::NodPtr(CMwNod@ nod)
uint64 visPtr = Kinematic::NodPtr(vis); // vis is CSceneCharVis@ (not a CMwNod subclass)
```

## Repro
```angelscript
interface ICounter { uint get_Count() const; }
class RawCounter { }

uint Count(RawCounter@ c) { return 0; }

void Repro(ICounter@ ic) {
    Count(ic); // no implicit ICounter@ -> RawCounter@ conversion exists
}
```

## Compiler (game-side)
```
Minigames/HeadPose.as (117, 18) :  ERR : No matching signatures to 'SmPlayerVis::PlayersLength(SmPlayerVis::ISmPlayerVisMgr@&)'
Minigames/HeadPose.as (158, 25) :  ERR : No matching signatures to 'Kinematic::NodPtr(CSceneCharVis@&)'
Script compilation failed!
```

## LSP today
Silent on both call sites. The symbols themselves resolve (both are used elsewhere in the same tree without complaint), so this is the call-vs-signature compatibility check that is missing, not name resolution.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Argument handle type that converts to no overload parameter → error naming the callee and the argument type (match the game's `No matching signatures to 'F(T@&)'` shape)
- [ ] Upcast to a base/interface parameter stays silent
- [ ] Explicit `cast<To>(arg)` at the call site stays silent
- [ ] Primitive mismatches with no implicit conversion (e.g. `int` arg to `string` param) — verify parity with the game and match it
- [ ] Error position points at the argument expression, not the callee
- [ ] Regression fixture

## Notes
Cascading effect to consider: once the call fails, AngelScript reports every later use of the would-be-initialized local as `No matching symbol` — we only need the primary diagnostic. This class hid behind `#if DEPENDENCY_X && DEPENDENCY_Y` guards in the source plugin for weeks because the guarded state never occurred in dev; the LSP never type-checked that code until the guards were removed.